11. Phase 6: Payments and reconciliation
Goal: the system records only money that was really received, once, in the right amount, against the right invoice.
Risks blocked: fake transfer screenshots and alerts, a customer's browser redirect treated as proof of payment, duplicate webhooks double-counting, wrong-amount payments, overpayment, and refunds that erase history.
Build order. Manual transfer verification first, because most of your customers will pay by transfer. Paystack second, behind a flag. Never test with live money.
Manual transfer verification checklist (built into the confirm screen)
The 'Confirm payment' button stays disabled until the person ticks every item:
1. The money is visible in the business bank account or official bank statement. A screenshot or an SMS alert alone is not proof.
2. The amount matches the invoice or the agreed instalment exactly.
3. The sender name or the narration matches the customer or the invoice number.
4. The credit date and the bank reference are typed in.
5. The confirming user is recorded. By default only the owner confirms (decision 15).
A payment starts as awaiting_verification. Only a confirmed payment counts toward the balance.
